@startuml

title The states of a firing

hide empty description

state "the firing machine" as f {
    state "start" as f_start <<start>>
    state "starting" as f_starting
    state "succeeded" as f_ok
    state "failed" as f_bad
}

f_start --> f_starting : the record is written
f_starting --> f_ok : the action returns
f_starting --> f_bad : the action refuses, or no handler\nanswers the action kind
f_ok --> [*]
f_bad --> [*]

note right of f_starting
    The record is written before the
    action runs, so a firing that fails
    still leaves a row behind.
end note

note bottom of f_bad
    The reason travels with the record.
    The next occurrence is a new firing,
    because the scheduler does not retry.
end note

legend bottom
    A firing ends in succeeded or failed.
    A point that is never fired leaves no firing at all, and a firing
    whose record cannot be written never reaches its action.
end legend

@enduml
